course: dictionary
lesson: 5335
title: Khaki: a colour and a cloth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Khaki is a dull yellow-brown or greenish-brown colour, a bit like sand or dry grass. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. In «khaki shorts» and «khaki trousers», khaki tells you the colour of the clothes.
explain: Khaki can also be a strong cotton cloth used for army uniforms. Clues like "soldiers" or "uniform" tell you the cloth meaning is the right one.
explain: Khaki can be an adjective, describing something, or a noun. Khakis, in the US, are trousers made of this cloth. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Watch for capital letters. In «some breeds like the Khaki Campbell lay nearly 300 a year», Khaki Campbell is the name of a breed of duck, not a colour. A capital letter and a name are clues too.
fact: Khaki means a dull yellow-brown or greenish-brown colour.
fact: «khaki shorts» and «khaki trousers» show khaki as the colour of clothes.
fact: Khaki is also a strong cotton cloth, used for army uniforms.
fact: In the US, khakis means trousers made of khaki cloth.
fact: Soldiers in the First World War wore khaki so they were harder to see.
exercise: «She wore khaki shorts to the park.» What colour were her shorts? | *Dull yellow-brown or greenish-brown | Sky blue | Snow white | Bright pink
exercise: Why did soldiers in the First World War wear khaki? | To shine in the sun | *To be harder to see | To keep their feet dry | To look smart at parties
exercise: Ella reads about a Khaki Campbell laying eggs. What is a Khaki Campbell? | A colour of paint | A kind of sandwich | *A breed of duck | A soldier's cap
test: In «the soldiers wore khaki uniforms», what does khaki tell us? | *What colour or kind the uniforms were | How many uniforms there were | When they wore them | Who the soldiers were
test: What does «khakis» mean in the US? | A kind of shoe | A brown bird | A type of soldier | *Trousers made of khaki cloth
exam: Which sentence uses khaki as a colour, not a name? | «Ella met a Khaki Campbell at the farm.» | *«Her khaki jacket was warm.» | «Khaki Campbells lay many eggs.» | «The Khaki Campbell is a breed of duck.»
